── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #187  overall: compliant
Spec: IOPMP reference model previously merged address-match and permission-grant into a single enum, risking hardware that authorizes DMA transactions solely on address-range hit without validating per-entry R/W/X bits
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/reset/thead,th1520-reset.h:30-30  N/A (macro definition)
      This is a preprocessor macro defining TH1520_RESET_ID_AUDSYS_IOPMP (reset ID 19). It is a constant used by the reset controller driver to locate the correct reset line for the IOPMP hardware block. This has no relationship to IOPMP entry permission checking, MATCH/GRANT semantics, or R/W/X validation per RRID. It is purely a hardware-reset identifier and cannot contribute to the described vulnerability.
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/clock/thead,th1520-clk-ap.h:121-126  N/A (macro definitions)
      These are six preprocessor macros (CLK_IOPMP_VOSYS_DPU_PCLK through CLK_IOPMP_GPU_ACLK, IDs 22-27) defining clock-gating constants for IOPMP interface clocks on the TH1520 SoC. They are opaque integer identifiers consumed by the Common Clock Framework and the IOPMP driver to reference specific clock gates. They encode no IOPMP security logic, no permission-checking semantics, and no MATCH/GRANT distinction. The vulnerability concerns the IOPMP hardware's internal transaction-authorization flow, which is unrelated to which clock ID constants exist.
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1250-1261  N/A (file-scope CCU_GATE definitions)
      These six CCU_GATE() macro invocations define clock gate hardware structures (iopmp_vosys_dpu_pclk through iopmp_gpu_aclk) — concrete clock-provider objects backed by registers in the video-PLL clock controller. Three of the six IOPMP clocks (dpu1_aclk, dpu_aclk, gpu_aclk) are marked CLK_IS_CRITICAL, meaning the kernel will never gate them off, which is arguably security-positive (IOPMP interface clocks cannot be accidentally disabled). None of these structures implement or influence IOPMP entry matching, RRID-based permission checking, or R/W/X validation. They are purely clock-gate register abstractions.
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1402-1407  N/A (vosys_clks[] array)
      These six &iopmp_* clock-gate pointer entries register the IOPMP clock structures into the vosys_clks[] provider array, making them discoverable by the Common Clock Framework (CCF). This is the standard Linux clock-provider registration pattern. The array entries are statically initialized at compile time; there is no runtime logic here that could conditionally skip or alter IOPMP permission checking. The registration mechanism has no semantic overlap with the IOPMP MATCH/GRANT conflation vulnerability.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #187  overall: potential_risk
Spec: IOPMP reference model previously merged address-match and permission-grant into a single enum, risking hardware that authorizes DMA transactions solely on address-range hit without validating per-entry R/W/X bits
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:220-271  sbi_domain_check_addr
      Correctly implements the two-phase MATCH-then-GRANT pattern. Phase 1: address-range match at L258 (rstart <= addr && addr <= rend). Phase 2: independent permission validation at L266 ((rrwx & rwx) == rwx). The MMIO mismatch guard at L264-265 provides an additional safety gate. The permission mask rrwx is extracted from the region flags using the caller's privilege mode (L250-253), so permissions are validated per-access-type and per-mode. This is the canonical reference implementation of the security requirement.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:497-526  sbi_domain_check_addr_range
      Correctly chains two independent phases: address-range match via find_region() at L509 (pure address lookup, no permission check), then full MATCH+GRANT via sbi_domain_check_addr() at L513 (which independently validates address bounds AND R/W/X permissions). The find_next_subset_region() at L516 correctly handles overlapping/subset regions. If L513 were omitted, this would be the canonical conflation bug, but the code correctly enforces both steps for every byte-range segment.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:326-342  find_region
