PR #187  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: IOPMP reference model previously merged address-match and permission-grant into a single enum, risking hardware that authorizes DMA transactions solely on address-range hit without validating per-entry R/W/X bits
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:589-608  (global namespace)
      Defines CSR_PMPCFG0-3 and CSR_PMPADDR0-15 as raw CSR numbers, but the entire FreeBSD kernel tree contains zero definitions for PMP permission bit constants (R/W/X/L). Any kernel code that reasons about PMP protection can only check that PMP entries exist, not what permissions they grant. This makes it structurally impossible to separate 'entry matched this address' from 'entry grants this access type' -- the exact MATCH==GRANT conflation targeted by the spec.
  [RISK] sys/dev/iommu/iommu_gas.h:45-57  (global namespace)
      IOMMU_MAP_ENTRY_READ (0x1000), IOMMU_MAP_ENTRY_WRITE (0x2000), and IOMMU_MAP_ENTRY_MAP (0x0004) are independent bitflags in a single u_int 'flags' field. Nothing in the type system prevents code from checking only MAP without checking READ/WRITE. The KASSERT at busdma_iommu.c:653 confirms this pattern exists in practice -- it asserts MAP is set but does not assert that READ or WRITE are consistent with the map's intended direction.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/busdma_bounce.c:93-104  struct bus_dmamap
      The bus_dmamap struct stores DMAMAP_COULD_BOUNCE and DMAMAP_FROM_DMAMEM flags but has NO field to record whether BUS_DMA_NOWRITE was set at dmamap_load time. This is the root-cause structural gap: without persisted direction state, no later operation on the map can distinguish 'buffer is mapped' from 'device is authorized to write.' The data structure itself conflates mapping existence with bidirectional permission.
  [RISK] sys/dev/iommu/busdma_iommu.h:45-59  struct bus_dmamap_iommu
      The bus_dmamap_iommu struct has no dedicated field recording BUS_DMA_NOWRITE or the effective IOMMU_MAP_ENTRY_WRITE state. Permission bits only live in the linked map_entries (each entry's flags field), reachable indirectly. The invariant that all entries in a single dmamap share the same R/W permission set is not enforced in code -- if entries were split/merged, the direction intent could be lost. A dedicated field would make the authorization intent explicit.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/busdma_bounce.c:627-717  bounce_bus_dmamap_load_buffer
      Accepts BUS_DMA_NOWRITE in the 'flags' parameter (line 629) but only uses it for page-counting (line 642) and never extracts or stores it. The sync_list is built from address contiguity alone (lines 684-703) with zero direction discrimination. Every DMA load is treated as bidirectional regardless of caller intent. Address-range mapping implicitly 'grants' both read and write to the device -- the precise MATCH==GRANT conflation the spec prohibits.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/busdma_bounce.c:828-920  bounce_bus_dmamap_sync
